   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to renaming\nagency building one, agency building two, agency building three and agency\nbuilding four, located at the empire state plaza",
    "basePrintNo": "S4722",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4722-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4.07, Arts & Cul L",
    "lawSection": "Arts and Cultural Affairs Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4722\n\nSPONSOR: FAHY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to renam-\ning agency building one, agency building two, agency building three and\nagency building four, located at the empire state plaza\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo rename agency buildings one through four at the Empire State Plaza.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 12 to section 4.07 of the arts and\ncultural affairs law to provide a process for renaming agency building\none through four on the Empire State Plaza.\n\nSection 2 is the after enactment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Governor Nelson A. Rockefeller Empire State Plaza is one of the most\nspectacular capital centers in the country. It contains major buildings\nlike the Egg Performance Center, Cultural Education Center as well as\noffice space for thousands of state employees\n\nMany of the Plaza buildings are named after famous people including\nErastus Corning, the longest tenured mayor of any large city the United\nStates (Corning Tower) and Robert Abrams , a long time State Attorney\nGeneral, (The Robert, Abrams Building for Law and Justice ).The four\nbuildings that house thousands of state employees are named 1,2,3, and\n4. The re-naming of these buildings is a great opportunity to honor New\nYork State's history.\n\nThis bill sets up a process for the selection of the names by using an\nexisting Commission. The Commission will make recommendations river to\nthe Governor. She will be the ultimate decision maker.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOne hundred and eighty days after enactment\n",
